=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Every layer of the reported outage was tested directly inside the lab and found healthy. From host_1_1_1_1, ICMP pings to web_server_2 (10.200.0.5) and web_server_3 (10.200.0.6) complete with 0% packet loss. Curl requests over HTTP to both web2.local and web3.local return HTTP 200 OK with the correct page content, as do web0.local and web1.local. DNS resolution for all four hostnames is working correctly via the dns_server at 10.200.0.2. Apache is actively listening on port 80 on all four web servers. iptables on web_server_2 and web_server_3 have empty rule sets — no firewall blocking is present.

The user's premise that switch_server_access has "obviously died" is also incorrect on two grounds: (1) the switch itself shows all six interfaces (eth0–eth5) in UP/LOWER_UP state, with eth0–eth4 bridged normally; (2) even if the switch had failed, all four web servers plus the DNS server share that same switch — a switch failure would take down web0.local and web1.local as well, not selectively two sites. The shared-switch argument actually disproves the switch-death hypothesis.

Root cause: There is no network fault. The symptom described in the ticket does not exist in the live lab — all paths from host_1_1_1_1 to web2.local and web3.local are fully functional at the IP, DNS, and HTTP layers.

Corrective fix: No configuration change is needed. The user should re-test from their actual client machine; the issue may be a browser cache problem, a stale local DNS entry, or a transient client-side condition that has already cleared.
=== END ===